Referential integrity ensures that relationships between tables remain consistent. It enforces that a foreign key must reference
an existing primary key in another table or be NULL. This prevents invalid or orphan records and maintains data integrity.

2. Explain Join in RDBMS/SQL with sample queries.

SELECT
    s.student_name,
    d.dept_name,
    sc.school_name
FROM student s
JOIN department d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id
JOIN school sc ON d.school_id = sc.school_id;

this will Returns ONLY rows where:
1)student has a valid department
2)department has a valid school
A JOIN in SQL is used to combine rows from two or more tables based on a related column. INNER JOIN returns only matching rows,
LEFT JOIN returns all rows from the left table, RIGHT JOIN returns all rows from the right table, and FULL JOIN returns all rows
from both tables.

